// `std.http.url` — percent-encoding and decoding (RFC 3986).
//
// The property that makes this native rather than a `.noe` helper: the transformation is over UTF-8
// BYTES. One character becomes several `%XX` escapes and has to be reassembled from them, which a
// scalar-based string surface cannot do — it would get ASCII right and mangle everything else.
// Pinned here so a future "simplification" into Noeta fails loudly on the non-ASCII rows.
// expect: stdout "a%20b%26c%3Dd"
// expect: stdout "a b&c=d"
// expect: stdout "%C3%A9"
// expect: stdout "café au lait"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "100% sure"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: exit 0
use std.http.url

// A component is encoded whole: the separators that would forge extra parameters are escaped.
echo url.encode("a b&c=d")
echo url.decode("a%20b%26c%3Dd")

// One character, two escapes — and back again as ONE character, not two.
echo url.encode("é")
echo url.decode("caf%C3%A9%20au%20lait")
echo url.decode("%C3%A9") == "é"

// A stray `%` is data. Refusing a query over one would be worse than reading it as written.
echo url.decode("100% sure")

// The round trip holds for every shape, including a literal `+` (which travels as `%2B`).
echo url.decode(url.encode("a+b é/c?d")) == "a+b é/c?d"
